WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.570
Results, goals, and an honest look at your working day.

2
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.910
This is the video for Module 1 of Productivity and Time Management at Work.

3
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:19.460
Ask people how their week went, and many will say, very busy. Busy describes activity: messages answered, calls taken, meetings attended.

4
00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:26.650
Productivity describes output. For a caterer, that is orders delivered on time and paid for.

5
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.290
This course is about growing output without adding hours.

6
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.720
And before you change anything, you will measure how your day is really spent.

7
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:46.690
Every role has three to five key results areas, the few outcomes that make everything else easier. Write them as results, not tasks.

8
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:57.330
Most key results depend on deep work. That is focused, demanding work that needs your experience and judgement, like writing a proposal or preparing a budget.

9
00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:07.150
Shallow work is easier, like forwarding a file. If a smart new colleague could do it well after one week of training, it is probably shallow.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.330
Shallow work arrives all day by itself. Deep work only happens if you protect time for it.

11
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.780
Amaka works from 8 in the morning to 8 at night, but profit is flat.

12
00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:25.870
She lists everything she did on Tuesday. 14 tasks, including 3 hours on WhatsApp.

13
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:38.940
She writes her results areas, such as jobs delivered on time, new corporate clients, and cash collected. Only 5 of the 14 tasks served a result. None served new clients.

14
00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:53.680
So every weekday from 9 to 10:30 is now for deep work on one results area. Within the week she sends her company profile to 6 potential clients, work she had postponed for months while staying busy.

15
00:01:54.930 --> 00:02:05.610
A goal turns a results area into a target with a finish line. A SMART goal is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant to your results, and has a deadline.

16
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:12.830
Improve customer service is vague. Reply to every WhatsApp enquiry within 2 working hours is SMART.

17
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:22.340
An OKR is an objective plus key results. The objective is what you want, in plain inspiring words, with no number.

18
00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:29.370
The key results are three to five measurable signs that you got there, each with a number and a date.

19
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.500
Initiatives are the actions you take to move them.

20
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:42.750
The common mistake is measuring effort. Send 50 flyers is an activity. Get 10 new office enquiries is a result.

21
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.270
Her sales have been uneven. Her objective: build steady monthly sales from returning customers.

22
00:02:51.520 --> 00:03:00.270
Key result 1. 40 repeat customers by 30 September. Her sales book shows about 15 last quarter. That is her baseline.

23
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:08.090
Key result 2. An average order of 25000 naira or more, up from about 18000.

24
00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.030
Key result 3. 4 new collections announced on schedule.

25
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:18.240
Now every Friday she checks three numbers, instead of a general hope for more sales.

26
00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:28.580
Now measure your starting point, your baseline. For 3 normal days, record what you worked on in 30-minute blocks, or each time you switch task.

27
00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.970
Note which results area it served, and whether it was deep or shallow.

28
00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:45.440
Tally every interruption with a letter. W for WhatsApp, E for email, C for calls, P for a person, and S for self, like picking up your phone just to check.

29
00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:50.510
Then count focused deep-work hours, with zero or one interruption.

30
00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.580
Funke feels she never has time to write donor reports. She logs 3 days.

31
00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:07.580
She gets only 1 focused hour a day, and 23 interruptions a day. 31 of them in three days came from WhatsApp, mostly a busy staff group chat.

32
00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.620
54 percent of her time goes on results.

33
00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:22.070
She has 2 hours of meetings a day, so 6 hours left. Minus a 20 percent buffer for the unexpected, she can plan about 4.8 hours.

34
00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:27.600
Do not plan to 100 percent. The first urgent request would break the whole day.

35
00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:36.830
To remember. Measure yourself by output. Protect time for deep work. Write key results as outcomes with numbers and dates.

36
00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:42.470
Tally interruptions by source, and plan only to your real capacity, with a buffer.

37
00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:53.110
Now your turn. Write your key results areas, write one OKR for this quarter, and run your three-day baseline. Keep your numbers for Module 4.

38
00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.400
Continue with the lessons below.
